---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { author } from '@/utils/author';
---
<Layout title="About" description="What this site is, who it is for, and how it was made.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    This is a small, deliberately slow site. It exists because two ideas that rarely meet, a piece of
    speculative philosophy from the 1990s and the practical question of how to talk about AI risk, turn
    out to have something to say to each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wrote it for people who publish about AI doom. Researchers, forecasters, journalists, novelists,
    podcasters, and the many people who just repost. It argues that
    they now write for two audiences and should know it. It does not ask them to stop.
  </p>
  <h2>A note on method</h2>
  <p>
    I drafted the essay with a large language model. Given the subject, that seemed like an experiment worth
    running. The model was asked to help describe a feedback loop it is itself inside. Where
    the text makes claims about how models are trained or how they behave, it cites public research.
    Where it guesses, it says so.
  </p>
  <h2>Who wrote it</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={author.site}>Jeff Kazzee</a>. I run <a href={author.company}>{author.companyName}</a> and write about AI on
    <a href={author.substack}>Substack</a>. The quickest way to argue with me is on <a href={author.x}>X</a>. The
    source for this site is on <a href={author.github}>GitHub</a>, and pull requests that fix a bad citation are
    welcome.
  </p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong, a mischaracterized paper, a misattributed idea, a bad inference, the
    right response is to say so publicly. That is, after all, the thesis.
  </p>
</Layout>
